    "text": "<p>A rod with circular cross-section area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> and length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is wound uniformly with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>400</mn></math> turns of an insulated wire. If a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> flows in the wire windings, the total magnetic flux produced inside windings is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Wb</mi></math>. The relative permeability of the rod is</p><p>(Given : Permeability of vacuum&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)</p>",
